0,9,0,9,0,4,0,4,0,9,0,9,0,9,0],[0,9,9,9,0,9,0,9,9,9,0,9,0,9,9,9,0],[0,0,0,0,0,9,0,0,0,0,0,9,0,0,0,0,0]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-fable-5-1"],"explanation":"## What the grid is made of\n\nEach input is a 17×17 grid cut by two full rows and two full columns of one colour (2, 8, 1 in the demonstrations; 9 in the test) into a 3×3 arrangement of 5×5 panels. Inside the panels there is a second colour drawing shapes:\n\n- exactly one panel holds a complete 3×3 motif (a filled square, a plus, a hollow diamond, …),\n- the other panels hold either nothing or a *fragment* of that same motif, always sitting at the same position within its panel as the matching cells of the full motif (e.g. in pair 1 the panel at the top right has two cells of the 3×3 square at rows 1, cols 14–15; the centre-bottom panel has the four edge cells of the square's ring).\n\n## How the output is built\n\nComparing input and output, the only changes are `0 → divider colour`, and they fall exactly on the motif's footprint in every panel. In pair 2 the plus is drawn in 8 in all eight non-template panels; in pair 3 the diamond is drawn in 1. Cells that already carried the shape colour are left untouched — the fragments survive (pair 1 keeps its 1s at (1,14),(1,15); pair 3 keeps the 3s at (2,7), (2,15), (7,2), (8,1), …), and the template panel is unchanged because every motif cell there is already filled.\n\nSo the rule: **take the full motif from the panel with the most shape cells, and stamp it into every panel in the divider colour, writing only onto background cells.** I verified this with an inspect run: it reproduces all three train outputs cell for cell.\n\n## Applying it to the test input\n\nDividers are colour 9, the shape colour is 4. The top-left panel holds the complete motif: a 3×3 hollow square (8 cells). Fragments: two cells at the top of the top-middle panel, a hollow diamond (4 cells) and a bottom pair in the middle-left / middle-centre panels, and two side cells in the bottom-centre panel. The output stamps the hollow 3×3 square in 9 into all nine panels, leaving the existing 4s in place — exactly the grid printed by the inspect run.\n\nThe program emits one stage per panel so the construction is visible panel by panel.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    d = dividers(g)\n    div_colour = (d[\"rows\"] + d[\"cols\"])[0][1]\n    ps = panels(g)\n\n    # template = the panel carrying the most non-background cells (the complete motif)\n    best = max(ps, key=lambda b: int((crop(g, *b) != bg).sum()))\n    tpl = crop(g, *best) != bg\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    emit(out, \"Locate the dividers (colour %d) and the panel with the complete motif at box %s.\" % (div_colour, str(best)))\n\n    for (y0, x0, y1, x1) in ps:\n        sub = out[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1]\n        h = min(tpl.shape[0], sub.shape[0]); w = min(tpl.shape[1], sub.shape[1])\n        m = tpl[:h, :w] & (sub[:h, :w] == bg)\n        if m.any():\n            sub[:h, :w][m] = div_colour\n            emit(out, \"Stamp the motif into panel %s in the divider colour, keeping existing shape cells.\" % str((y0, x0, y1, x1)))\n\n    emit(out, \"All panels now carry the motif; fragments keep their original colour.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"panels":[{"source":"final explanation","text":" columns of one colour (2, 8, 1 in the demonstrations; 9 in the test) into a 3×3 arrangement of 5×5 panels."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Inside the panels there is a second colour drawing shapes:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- exactly one panel holds a complete 3×3 motif (a filled square, a plus, a hollow diamond, …),"}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- exactly one panel holds a complete 3×3 motif (a filled square, a plus, a hollow diamond, …),"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- the other panels hold either nothing or a *fragment* of that same motif, always sitting at the same position within its panel as the matching cells of the full motif (e.g. in pair 1 the panel at the top right has two cells of the 3×3 square at rows 1, cols 14–15; the centre-bottom panel has the four edge cells of the square's ring)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Comparing input and output, the only changes are `0 → divider colour`, and they fall exactly on the motif's footprint in every panel."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Comparing input and output, the only changes are `0 → divider colour`, and they fall exactly on the motif's footprint in every panel."}],"boolean":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- exactly one panel holds a complete 3×3 motif (a filled square, a plus, a hollow diamond, …),"}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"c16b703df2c7f655e324834136804a84471dc7225e540ce87fa79d6bff302c29","task_sha256":"a9114c57161e6b4e69a8fefa09f2de09c70ec4e77e562316b7d84f727d5ed4ca","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
